<div class="meta">Vocab 0025 · pairs with lesson 0026</div>
<h1>Vocab: Generate, Validate, Retry</h1>
<dl>
<dt>schema-as-contract</dt>
<dd>The model fills a defined structure the game already loads; it never
acts on the world directly. The map JSON and persona formats ARE the
generation API.</dd>

<dt>closed-world vocabulary</dt>
<dd>The prompt lists every id the model may use (pieces, parts, palettes,
traits) — generation composes existing content, never invents new kinds.</dd>

<dt>one-entrance rule</dt>
<dd>Exactly one path from generated output into the game, through the
validator. Any bypass makes the validator decoration.</dd>

<dt>retry feedback</dt>
<dd>The validator's error list appended verbatim to the next attempt's
prompt. Written model-grade: names, positions, and nearest valid
alternatives.</dd>

<dt>contract fault vs model fault</dt>
<dd>The diagnostic split: identical failures across models indict YOUR
format or prompt; scattered per-model failures indict the model. Tonight's
string-embedded personas and missing style families were contract faults.</dd>

<dt>unsatisfiable instruction</dt>
<dd>A prompt demand whose required information isn't in the prompt
("style-consistent" without family labels). No amount of emphasis fixes
it; the missing data does.</dd>

<dt>auto-repair</dt>
<dd>Silently fixing invalid output (nearest-id substitution, nudged
placements). Banned: it hides model quality and removes retry pressure.
Distinct from wrapper normalization, which strips packaging only.</dd>

<dt>validity rate</dt>
<dd>The bench's headline number: fraction of fixed-corpus generations that
pass the real validator (within the retry cap / on the first attempt).
The model is chosen by this measurement, never by label.</dd>

<dt>repairable vs strict load</dt>
<dd>Human files open with bad rows dropped and a toast; model output
either passes whole or fails plainly. Forgiveness for people, feedback
for models.</dd>
</dl>
